{{draft}}
{{AFC comment|1=Note: This draft started from a complete copy of [[Onmyōji]] as it has a lot of redlinks with claims I want to keep. I will trim down into just the claim on an individual thing later[[User:Immanuelle|'''Immanuel'''le]] ❤️💚💙 [[User_talk:Immanuelle|(talk to the cutest Wikipedian)]] 01:41, 25 April 2023 (UTC)}}
{{ill|Rōkoku Hakase|lt=Doctor of Water Clock|ja|漏刻博士}}[[ja:漏刻博士]] is position in the [[Ministry of the Center]] the chief timekeeper.<ref name="titsingh427">Titsingh, Isaac. (1834). {{Google books|18oNAAAAIAAJ|''Annales des empereurs du japon,'' p. 427.|page=427}}</ref><ref>https://academiccommons.columbia.edu/doi/10.7916/d8-3zya-0s65/download</ref><ref>https://academiccommons.columbia.edu/doi/10.7916/D8HD82PN/download</ref><ref>https://d-museum.kokugakuin.ac.jp/eos/detail/id=8943</ref><ref>https://bazhum.muzhp.pl/media/files/Analecta_Nipponica/Analecta_Nipponica-r2011-t1/Analecta_Nipponica-r2011-t1-s139-202/Analecta_Nipponica-r2011-t1-s139-202.pdf</ref>

Later, [[Emperor Tenmu]] was such a master of astrology and fugue that he took his own divination tools and told fortunes during the [[Jinshin War]]. He also had a deep knowledge of the yin-and-yang five phases philosophy, which led to the establishment of the Bureau of Onmyō and Japan's first [[observatory]] in 676, and the use of the term "onmyōji" in 685, which further increased the popularity of the yin-and-yang five phases philosophy. In 718, the [[Yōrō Code]] established the Bureau of Onmyō as an internal department of the Ministry of Center, and when it was stipulated that the positions of {{ill|Temmon Hakase|lt=Doctor of Astrology|ja|天文博士}}, {{ill|Onmyō Hakase|lt=Doctor of Onmyō|ja|陰陽博士}}, onmyōji, {{ill|Reki Hakase|lt=Doctor of Calendar|ja|暦博士}}, and {{ill|Rōkoku Hakase|lt=Doctor of Water Clock|ja|漏刻博士}} should be permanently assigned as technical officers, the Bureau of Onmyō became officially in charge of divination along with the [[turtle shell]] diviners who belonged to the [[Department of Divinities]].


'''Rōkoku Hakase''' ({{ill|漏刻博士|ja|漏刻博士}}) was a position within Japan's [[Ritsuryō system]], specifically under the {{ill|Onmyōryō|ja|陰陽寮}} bureau. The Japanese reading is "Tokitsukasa," and its Tang Chinese names included "Shijin" (司辰) and "Keiko Shi" (契壷司). The position had two regular appointees, with provisions for additional adjuncts. While formally ranked at {{ill|Junior Seventh Rank, Lower Grade|ja|従七位下}}, appointees of the {{ill|Fifth Rank|ja|五位}} and {{ill|Sixth Rank|ja|六位}} were not uncommon (as noted in the {{ill|Shokugen Shō|ja|職源鈔}}).

The Rōkoku Hakase oversaw {{ill|守辰丁|ja|守辰丁}} (20 individuals selected from the {{ill|Shichō|ja|仕丁}}) to monitor the {{ill|Water clock|ja|漏刻}}, measure time, and command the守辰丁 to strike a bell within the Onmyōryō at set intervals. During {{ill|Imperial visits|ja|行幸}}, one Rōkoku Hakase and twelve守辰丁 were required to accompany the emperor along with the water clock.

Although Tang China also had a similar office of "Rōkoku Hakase," in China the practical management of water clocks was handled by officials such as "Keiko Se" (挈壺正), "Shijin" (司辰), and "Rōkoku Tenji" (漏刻典事), while the Rōkoku Hakase focused on the education of students (Rōkoku Sei, 漏刻生). In contrast, Japan did not establish equivalent positions like Keiko Se or Shijin, nor did it have a system of Rōkoku Sei students. Thus, Japanese Rōkoku Hakase directly managed water clocks and did not engage in teaching responsibilities, unlike counterparts such as the {{ill|Tenmon Hakase|ja|天文博士}} and {{ill|Reki Hakase|ja|暦博士}}, who combined practical and educational roles.<ref>Masashi Kinoshita, ''Kodai no Rōkoku to Jikoku Seido'' (Ancient Water Clocks and the Timekeeping System), Yoshikawa Kōbunkan, 2020, ISBN 978-4-642-04657-2, pp. 307–312.</ref>

The use of water clocks in Japan is thought to have ceased relatively early. By the {{ill|Insei period|ja|院政期}}, the position of Rōkoku Hakase became primarily ceremonial, often serving members of the {{ill|Kamo no Asomi clan|ja|賀茂朝臣氏}}, the {{ill|Abe clan|ja|阿倍氏}}, or their recommended students. {{ill|Sugano no Sukechika|ja|菅野季親}} and {{ill|Sugano no Suketaka|ja|菅野季長}} are said to have earned the title not for expertise in water clocks but for their mastery of {{ill|Nenshaku divination|ja|年筮}}. 

Water clocks were also established in remote regions such as {{ill|Dazaifu|ja|大宰府}} and {{ill|Mutsu Province|ja|陸奥国}} (later expanded to include {{ill|Dewa Province|ja|出羽国}}), but no Rōkoku Hakase were assigned; instead,守辰丁 managed these clocks.

